## Tutorials

❮ Java Keywords

Theassertkeyword evaluates a boolean expression and throws anAssertionErrorexception if the expression evaluates tofalse. When the exception is thrown we say that the assertion failed.

An optional expression can be added which will be used as the exception message if the assertion fails.

Assertions are disabled by default.assertstatements are ignored unless assertions are enabled.

The purpose of assertions is to clearly mark where a program is doing something unintended when debugging and testing a program.

❮ Java Keywords

### Code Examples

```
Example
Use assert to test the truth value of expressions:
public class Main {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    // Enable assertions
    ClassLoader loader = ClassLoader.getSystemClassLoader();
    loader.setDefaultAssertionStatus(true);

    // Run the assert example
    AssertExample example = new AssertExample();
    example.run();
  }
}

class AssertExample {
  public void run() {
    int a = 12;
    try {
      assert a == 12; // Assertion without a fail message
      assert a == 12 : "a is not 12";
      assert a == 15 : "a is not 15";
    } catch (AssertionError e) {
      System.out.println(e.getMessage());
    }
  }
}
